## Pipeline Example

```java
List<Employee> employees = getAllEmployees();

// Find names of top 3 earners in Engineering, salary formatted
List<String> result = employees.stream()
    .filter(e -> "Engineering".equals(e.getDepartment()))  // intermediate
    .sorted(Comparator.comparingDouble(Employee::getSalary).reversed())  // intermediate
    .limit(3)                                               // intermediate
    .map(e -> e.getName() + " ($" + e.getSalary() + ")")  // intermediate
    .collect(Collectors.toList());                          // terminal — triggers execution
```

## flatMap — One-to-Many

```java
List<Order> orders = getOrders();

// All unique product IDs across all orders
Set<Long> productIds = orders.stream()
    .flatMap(order -> order.getItems().stream())  // Order → Stream<OrderItem>
    .map(OrderItem::getProductId)
    .collect(Collectors.toSet());

// Flatten nested lists
List<List<Integer>> nested = List.of(List.of(1,2,3), List.of(4,5), List.of(6));
List<Integer> flat = nested.stream()
    .flatMap(Collection::stream)  // List<Integer> → Stream<Integer>
    .collect(Collectors.toList());  // [1,2,3,4,5,6]
```

## reduce — Custom Aggregation

```java
// Sum: explicit reduce
int total = IntStream.rangeClosed(1, 100).reduce(0, Integer::sum);

// String concatenation
Optional<String> joined = Stream.of("a", "b", "c")
    .reduce((a, b) -> a + ", " + b);  // Optional[a, b, c]

// Product with identity
double product = DoubleStream.of(1.5, 2.0, 3.0)
    .reduce(1.0, (a, b) -> a * b);  // 9.0
```

## Short-Circuit Operations

```java
List<String> names = List.of("Alice", "Bob", "Charlie", "David");

// Stops after first match — doesn't process all elements
Optional<String> first = names.stream()
    .filter(n -> n.startsWith("C"))
    .findFirst();  // Optional[Charlie]

// anyMatch stops at first true
boolean hasLong = names.stream().anyMatch(n -> n.length() > 5);  // true (Charlie)

// allMatch stops at first false
boolean allShort = names.stream().allMatch(n -> n.length() < 4);  // false (stops at Alice)
```

## Collectors

```java
// groupingBy — Map<K, List<V>>
Map<String, List<Employee>> byDept = employees.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Employee::getDepartment));

// groupingBy with downstream collector
Map<String, Double> avgSalaryByDept = employees.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
        Employee::getDepartment,
        Collectors.averagingDouble(Employee::getSalary)
    ));

// Joining
String names = employees.stream()
    .map(Employee::getName)
    .collect(Collectors.joining(", ", "[", "]"));
// [Alice, Bob, Charlie]

// partitioningBy
Map<Boolean, List<Employee>> seniorAndJunior = employees.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.partitioningBy(e -> e.getYearsExp() >= 5));
// {true=[seniors], false=[juniors]}
```

## Advanced Collector Examples

```java
// groupingBy with downstream — avg salary per dept
Map<String, Double> avgByDept = employees.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
        Employee::getDepartment,
        Collectors.averagingDouble(Employee::getSalary)
    ));

// nested groupingBy — dept → role → count
Map<String, Map<String, Long>> deptRoleCount = employees.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
        Employee::getDepartment,
        Collectors.groupingBy(Employee::getRole, Collectors.counting())
    ));

// Collectors.mapping — transform before collecting
Map<String, Set<String>> deptEmails = employees.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
        Employee::getDepartment,
        Collectors.mapping(Employee::getEmail, Collectors.toSet())
    ));
```

## Collectors.teeing (Java 12+)

```java
// Compute min and max in a single pass
record MinMax(Employee min, Employee max) {}

MinMax result = employees.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.teeing(
        Collectors.minBy(Comparator.comparingDouble(Employee::getSalary)),
        Collectors.maxBy(Comparator.comparingDouble(Employee::getSalary)),
        (min, max) -> new MinMax(min.orElseThrow(), max.orElseThrow())
    ));

// Count and sum in one pass to compute average
record SumCount(double sum, long count) {
    double average() { return count == 0 ? 0 : sum / count; }
}

SumCount sc = employees.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.teeing(
        Collectors.summingDouble(Employee::getSalary),
        Collectors.counting(),
        SumCount::new
    ));
System.out.println(sc.average());
```

## Custom Collector

```java
// Collector that produces a sorted, deduplicated, joined string
Collector<String, TreeSet<String>, String> sortedJoining = Collector.of(
    TreeSet::new,          // supplier: mutable container
    TreeSet::add,          // accumulator: how to add element
    (s1, s2) -> { s1.addAll(s2); return s1; },  // combiner: for parallel streams
    set -> String.join(", ", set)  // finisher: TreeSet → String
    // Characteristics: none — maintains order, not concurrent
);

String result = Stream.of("banana", "apple", "cherry", "apple")
    .collect(sortedJoining);  // "apple, banana, cherry"
```

## When Parallel Helps

```java
// CPU-intensive, large dataset — parallel is beneficial
List<String> results = largeList.parallelStream()  // 100k+ elements
    .filter(s -> complexPredicate(s))   // CPU-heavy check
    .map(s -> heavyTransform(s))        // CPU-heavy transform
    .collect(Collectors.toList());
```

## When Parallel Hurts

```java
// WRONG: I/O inside parallel stream — blocks ForkJoin worker threads
List<UserDTO> users = userIds.parallelStream()
    .map(id -> httpClient.getUser(id))  // blocking HTTP call!
    .collect(Collectors.toList());

// CORRECT: CompletableFuture with dedicated thread pool for I/O
Executor ioPool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(20);
List<CompletableFuture<UserDTO>> futures = userIds.stream()
    .map(id -> CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> httpClient.getUser(id), ioPool))
    .toList();
List<UserDTO> users = futures.stream()
    .map(CompletableFuture::join)
    .toList();
```

## Race Condition in Parallel Stream

```java
// WRONG: non-thread-safe shared state
List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();  // ArrayList is not thread-safe
IntStream.rangeClosed(1, 1000).parallel()
    .forEach(result::add);  // concurrent modifications → data corruption / missing elements

// CORRECT: use collector
List<Integer> result = IntStream.rangeClosed(1, 1000).parallel()
    .boxed()
    .collect(Collectors.toList());  // thread-safe collection

// CORRECT: thread-safe accumulator
long count = IntStream.rangeClosed(1, 1000).parallel()
    .filter(n -> n % 2 == 0)
    .count();  // terminal op handles thread safety
```

## Performance Considerations

```java
// LinkedList splits poorly (no random access) — parallel won't help
List<Integer> linked = new LinkedList<>(largeList);
linked.parallelStream().filter(...)  // poor parallelism — better to use sequential

// ArrayList / arrays split efficiently
int[] array = IntStream.range(0, 1_000_000).toArray();
long sum = Arrays.stream(array).parallel().sum();  // excellent parallelism

// Custom ForkJoinPool for isolated parallel work
ForkJoinPool pool = new ForkJoinPool(4);  // 4 threads, not common pool
pool.submit(() ->
    largeList.parallelStream().forEach(item -> process(item))
).get();
```

## Lambda Syntax

```java
// Anonymous class (old way)
Runnable r1 = new Runnable() {
    @Override public void run() { System.out.println("Hello"); }
};

// Lambda (Java 8+)
Runnable r2 = () -> System.out.println("Hello");

// With parameter
Comparator<String> c = (a, b) -> a.length() - b.length();

// Multi-line
Function<Integer, String> f = n -> {
    if (n < 0) return "negative";
    if (n == 0) return "zero";
    return "positive";
};
```

## Built-in Functional Interfaces

```java
// Function<T, R>: T → R
Function<String, Integer> length = String::length;
Function<Integer, Integer> doubled = n -> n * 2;
Function<String, Integer> lengthDoubled = length.andThen(doubled);
System.out.println(lengthDoubled.apply("hello"));  // 10

// Predicate<T>: T → boolean
Predicate<String> isEmpty = String::isEmpty;
Predicate<String> isNotEmpty = isEmpty.negate();
Predicate<Integer> isPositive = n -> n > 0;
Predicate<Integer> isEven = n -> n % 2 == 0;
Predicate<Integer> isPositiveEven = isPositive.and(isEven);

// Consumer<T>: T → void
Consumer<String> print = System.out::println;
Consumer<String> printUpper = s -> System.out.println(s.toUpperCase());
print.andThen(printUpper).accept("hello");  // hello, HELLO

// Supplier<T>: () → T
Supplier<List<String>> listFactory = ArrayList::new;
List<String> list = listFactory.get();

// BiFunction<T, U, R>: (T, U) → R
BiFunction<String, Integer, String> repeat = (s, n) -> s.repeat(n);
System.out.println(repeat.apply("ab", 3));  // ababab
```

## Closure — Captured Variables

```java
void demo() {
    String prefix = "Hello";  // effectively final
    Function<String, String> greet = name -> prefix + ", " + name;
    // prefix is captured — must be effectively final (not reassigned)

    // prefix = "Hi";  // This would cause compile error: variable used in lambda must be final
}
```

## Custom @FunctionalInterface

```java
@FunctionalInterface
public interface TriFunction<A, B, C, R> {
    R apply(A a, B b, C c);
}

TriFunction<Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer> clamp =
    (value, min, max) -> Math.min(Math.max(value, min), max);
```

## Creating Optional

```java
Optional<String> empty   = Optional.empty();
Optional<String> present = Optional.of("hello");    // throws NPE if null
Optional<String> maybe   = Optional.ofNullable(null); // empty if null, safe
```

## Extracting Values — Correct Ways

```java
Optional<User> maybeUser = userRepo.findByEmail("test@example.com");

// orElse: always evaluates fallback (use for cheap values)
User user = maybeUser.orElse(User.anonymous());

// orElseGet: lazy — only evaluates if empty (use for expensive creation)
User user = maybeUser.orElseGet(() -> createDefaultUser());

// orElseThrow: throw if empty (use when absence is exceptional)
User user = maybeUser.orElseThrow(() -> new UserNotFoundException(email));

// ifPresent: side effect only if value exists
maybeUser.ifPresent(u -> log.info("Found user: {}", u.getName()));

// ifPresentOrElse (Java 9+)
maybeUser.ifPresentOrElse(
    u -> log.info("Found: {}", u.getName()),
    () -> log.warn("User not found")
);
```

## Transforming with map and flatMap

```java
// map: transform the value if present
Optional<String> email = maybeUser.map(User::getEmail);
Optional<Integer> nameLength = maybeUser.map(User::getName).map(String::length);

// flatMap: use when transformation itself returns Optional
// (avoids Optional<Optional<T>>)
Optional<Address> address = maybeUser.flatMap(User::getAddress);
// User::getAddress returns Optional<Address>
// Without flatMap you'd get Optional<Optional<Address>>

// Chaining — find city name if user has an address
String city = maybeUser
    .flatMap(User::getAddress)
    .map(Address::getCity)
    .orElse("Unknown");
```

## Anti-Patterns

```java
// WRONG: defeats the purpose of Optional
if (maybeUser.isPresent()) {
    User user = maybeUser.get();  // still using get — no benefit over null check
}

// WRONG: Optional as field
public class User {
    private Optional<String> nickname;  // Don't! Use String nickname nullable instead
}

// WRONG: Optional as parameter
public void process(Optional<String> input) {  // Don't! Use @Nullable String or overload
}

// RIGHT: use directly in stream
List<Address> addresses = users.stream()
    .map(User::getAddress)      // Stream<Optional<Address>>
    .flatMap(Optional::stream)  // Stream<Address> — removes empty optionals (Java 9+)
    .toList();
```

## Four Types

```java
// 1. Static method reference: ClassName::staticMethod
// Lambda: n -> String.valueOf(n)
Function<Integer, String> toStr = String::valueOf;
// Lambda: s -> Integer.parseInt(s)
Function<String, Integer> parse = Integer::parseInt;

List<Integer> numbers = List.of("1","2","3").stream()
    .map(Integer::parseInt)
    .toList();

// 2. Instance method of a particular (bound) object: object::instanceMethod
String prefix = "LOG: ";
UnaryOperator<String> logger = prefix::concat;  // captures 'prefix' object
List<String> logged = messages.stream().map(prefix::concat).toList();

// 3. Instance method of arbitrary (unbound) object: ClassName::instanceMethod
// Lambda: str -> str.toUpperCase()
Function<String, String> upper = String::toUpperCase;
// Lambda: s -> s.length()
Function<String, Integer> length = String::length;

List<String> upperNames = names.stream()
    .map(String::toUpperCase)
    .sorted(Comparator.comparingInt(String::length))
    .toList();

// 4. Constructor reference: ClassName::new
Supplier<ArrayList<String>> listFactory = ArrayList::new;
Function<String, StringBuilder> sbFactory = StringBuilder::new;

List<User> users = names.stream()
    .map(User::new)  // User(String name) constructor
    .toList();

// Useful with Collectors
Map<String, List<String>> grouped = names.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
        String::toUpperCase,
        Collectors.toCollection(ArrayList::new)  // constructor ref as supplier
    ));
```

## When to Prefer Method Reference vs Lambda

```java
// Prefer method reference — clean and obvious
employees.stream().map(Employee::getName).forEach(System.out::println);

// Keep as lambda — has logic beyond a single call
employees.stream()
    .filter(e -> e.getSalary() > 50000 && e.isActive())
    .map(e -> e.getFirstName() + " " + e.getLastName())
    .forEach(System.out::println);
```
